# @PROJECT@ — repository rules

This file **delegates**. A leaf that restates a rule cannot inherit fixes to it
(`recomp-ai-rules/CONTRIBUTING.md`). Everything here is either a pointer or a fact
that is true only of this repository.

## Precedence

1. `../recomp-ai-rules/PRINCIPLES.md` — system-agnostic, authoritative.
2. `../recomp-ai-rules/N64/` — platform addenda.
3. `n64lle/CLAUDE.md` and the n64lle doc set — the framework's own rules.
4. This file.

A lower level may add detail or be stricter; it may not weaken a level above it.
Where they conflict, the lower file is the bug — say so and fix it there.

## Session start

Read `docs/STATUS.md` first: it carries the measured state and the ordered open
items. Then n64lle's `HANDOFF.md` and `docs/evidence/KNOWN-ISSUES.md` for the
framework's own open bugs.

Then verify the tools before trusting them (PRINCIPLES.md, "Tool Skepticism").
Confirm which ROM, which build tree, and which flags a run actually used before
reading anything into its output. The worked example this family keeps
rediscovering: a harvest run without `N64_RSP_EXEC=1` reports a clean result that
is a ~70 % under-harvest, and identical coverage at two window sizes looks like
evidence the game is stuck when it is evidence the flag is missing.

## Facts specific to this repository

- **The framework is pre-built out of tree, not `add_subdirectory`d.** n64lle's
  `bench/` and `n64ref/` still resolve paths through `CMAKE_SOURCE_DIR`, which
  breaks under nesting. Build with `tools/build_framework.sh`, never a bare
  `cmake` on `n64lle/`.
  `tools/build_framework.sh` is a SHIM onto `n64lle/tools/build_framework.sh`,
  which the framework owns and every port shares. Do not add build logic to
  the shim: a private per-port copy is what left seven of nine N64 ports
  building without `-DN64LLE_RSP_CENSUS=1`, which silently compiled their
  generated-RSP tier to nothing (2026-09-15). Fixes go upstream.
- **There is no host source in this repo, on purpose.** The executable comes from
  one `n64lle_add_runtime_target()` call; the host lives in n64lle
  `runtime/host/`. If the launcher, input, audio or run loop needs a change, it
  goes UPSTREAM — never copied in here, because a copy cannot inherit a fix.
- **`game.toml` is hand-maintained and no program here writes it.** User settings
  go to `settings.toml` beside the executable. Precedence is
  `environment > CLI > settings.toml > game.toml > built-in`. A new runtime key
  gets tagged `[HOST]` in `game.toml`, and `@SLUG@_contract` (which runs
  `@EXE@ --check-config`) is the gate that catches an edit the reader cannot
  represent.
- **`N64_RSP_EXEC=1` is required for any graphics** in the harvest and the frame
  probe. RSP execution defaults off in n64lle's device model; the host turns it
  on itself from `game.toml`.
- **Timing claims come from `@SLUG@-bench`** — the paired, in-process tier A/B —
  never from a single-shot run and never as a cross-session absolute. State
  whether the RSP and rasterizer were live; `bench_driver` does not enable them,
  so its ratio is CPU-only.
- **Nothing here is graded against hardware.** "Correct" in this repo means
  "correct against the interpreter". Do not upgrade that wording.
- **`generated/` and `roms/` are gitignored.** Never commit either, and never
  commit a ROM byte anywhere.
- **The submodules stay pristine.** A framework change goes upstream in n64lle or
  into a committed `.patch` applied at CMake time — never an edit inside
  `n64lle/` or `recomp-ui/`.

## Hard stops that gate an action here

Restated only because each gates something you might otherwise do before reading
upstream. The reasoning lives in `recomp-ai-rules/PRINCIPLES.md`.

- **Resolve dispatch misses after every run, before any other debugging.**
- **No stubs.** Never synthesize a result to get past uncovered code.
- **Never edit generated output.** Fix the recompiler, runtime or `game.toml`,
  regenerate.
- **First divergence, and WRONG vs MISSING.** Classify before tracing: a wrong
  value has a writer; a missing effect has none, so ask why the producer never ran.
- **Screenshot before asserting anything about visible state.**
  `@SLUG@-frame-probe` is the tool. Ring counters are instrument state, not pixels.
- **Never pause or step to synchronize two observers.** Free-run and query
  always-on rings.
- **Gameplay verdicts belong to the owner.** Anything needing a real controller,
  or a human judgement about what is on screen, is not the assistant's to give.
